The following is the consolidated master prompt. It is intended to be supplied to a capable game-design, concept-art or production-planning model. It requests a complete design package and asset specifications, not code and not implementation.

MASTER PROMPT (copy from here)

You are the lead game director, senior cricket rules designer, gameplay systems designer, sports physicist, multiplayer systems architect, art director, character animator, UI/UX director and technical producer for an original mobile-first browser cricket game currently titled CREASE CLASH.

Your task is to create a complete, internally consistent production blueprint for the game. Do not write code, do not build a prototype, do not generate executable project files and do not claim that anything has been implemented. This assignment is strictly for product requirements, game design, system architecture, rules, interaction design, asset planning and visual-generation prompts.

A. Non-negotiable product identity

Create a skill-based cricket simulation for mobile and desktop browsers. The core mode is real-time online 1v1 with five overs per innings. In the first innings one player bats and the other bowls; in the second innings the roles swap. AI controls fielders in the first release while the bowling player selects field presets and limited field adjustments. The game should feel like cricket, not a random arcade batting game.

The supplied reference screenshot is the authoritative camera and on-field layout reference. LOCK the gameplay camera to an elevated, fixed, behind-the-bowler perspective, looking down the entire length of the pitch toward the striker. The pitch remains centered and vertical in the screen composition; the bowler is in the near foreground and striker at the far end. Keep both wickets, pitch markings, umpire, wicketkeeper, non-striker, relevant fielders, outfield and boundary readable. Do not change to side view, top-down or batter POV. Do not rotate, pan, zoom or cut the live gameplay camera during a ball. Preserve the reference's spatial composition and UI philosophy, but create a cleaner and more modern interface.

The environment must look like a real cricket ground: believable green outfield, brown prepared pitch, worn batting areas, correct white crease markings, wooden stumps and bails, leather cricket ball with stitched seam, boundary rope/line and cricket field markings. These ground/equipment elements should feel grounded and detailed. Player models must be original fictional designs and must not copy the exact people, uniforms, logos or identities in the reference. Visual quality should be polished, lightweight 2.5D/3D, not demanding photorealistic AAA graphics. Optimize for mobile browser performance and ball readability.

B. Absolute design principle

Every result must be explainable by the simulation. No shot button guarantees a direction, four, six or wicket. A shot button selects intent; physical interaction resolves outcome. Timing, delivery line, length, speed, movement, bounce, footwork, bat path, bat face, contact position, body balance, ball velocity, fielding position and running decisions must all matter. The game may use tiny deterministic seeded variation for natural feel, but random chance must never dominate player skill.

C. Specify the complete ball lifecycle

Document and diagram every step: pre-delivery setup, bowler run-up, gather, front-foot landing, release, ball flight, swing/seam/spin, pitch impact, bounce, post-bounce flight, batter footwork, bat trigger, backswing, downswing, bat/ball collision, edge/miss/solid contact, launch direction, ball flight/roll, catch attempt or fielding pickup, throw, running call, crease arrival, run-out check, boundary check, dismissal/extras check, dead-ball state, score update and next delivery. Define state machine, event order, data needed at each stage and failure/edge cases.

D. Build a coherent world-space physics specification

Use world coordinates X across the pitch, Y along the pitch and Z height. Run simulation in world space; the locked camera is only a projection. Specify ball position, linear and angular velocity, gravity, air drag, spin, swing force, seam deflection, pitch restitution, friction, bounce, roll and collision response. Define line and length using continuous world-space target coordinates with readable cricket labels. Describe how speed, release point, line, length, variation, seam orientation, spin axis and surface wear change ball path. Specify a deterministic model and how small seeded variation is bounded. Explain how the ball remains visible at all times without moving the camera.

E. Specify every bowling mechanic

Cover fast, medium pace and spin bowling. Include stock seam-up, outswing, inswing, off-cutter, leg-cutter, slower ball, yorker, full ball, full toss, bouncer, off-break, leg-break, googly, top-spinner, slider and arm ball. For each define intended use, release/action cues, flight behavior, bounce behavior, pace trade-offs, control difficulty and batter counterplay. Define speed, line, length, movement intensity, release timing/accuracy, bowling action, handedness, stamina/consistency if used, front-foot no-ball and wide detection. Define bowler run-up, gather, delivery stride, wrist/finger release and follow-through animations. Ensure a variation changes physical parameters, not just its label or animation.

F. Specify every batting mechanic and shot

Define ready stance, trigger, front-foot, back-foot, advance/charge, stay balanced and leave. Cover forward defence, back-foot defence, straight drive, on drive, off drive, cover drive, square drive, cut, late cut, pull, hook, flick/glance, sweep, paddle sweep, lofted drive and lofted leg-side shot. Optional advanced shots may be proposed separately. For every shot specify ideal line/length, ideal footwork, bat path, swing plane, bat-face behavior, contact window, follow-through, scoring potential and dismissal risks. Explain what happens when the player selects the wrong shot for a delivery.

G. Specify the complete bat-swing and contact system

Treat the bat as a moving physical object, not a static button result. Define stance, backswing, weight transfer, hip/shoulder rotation, downswing acceleration, bat angular velocity, contact window, blade/edge/toe/sweet-spot contact regions, bat face, vertical contact position, follow-through and recovery. Define how early, good, perfect and late timing affect geometry. Timing windows must change with incoming speed, bounce, delivery movement, footwork and chosen stroke. Shot direction must be calculated from bat face, swing vector, contact offset and incoming ball velocity. Include miss, inside edge, outside edge, top edge, bottom edge, mistimed contact, soft contact, solid contact and sweet-spot contact. Explain how a perfect timing label can still produce an edge or catch when the shot selection/geometry is poor.

H. Specify fielding, catching and wicketkeeping

Define fielder starting positions, field presets, restrictions, reaction delay, acceleration, route selection, interception, ground pickup, bobble, throw, direct hit, catch reach, jump, dive, boundary awareness and recovery. Fielders must not teleport or know the future. Explain catch physics and the difference between clean catch, juggle then control, drop, fingertip deflection, parry and miss. Include one-handed, two-handed, low, overhead, running-back, forward dive and side dive catches. Define wicketkeeper crouch, standing take, take to either side, stumping and bails removal. Specify how ball trajectory and fielder skill, rather than random proximity, determine catches.

I. Specify running between wickets

Define RUN/YES, STOP/NO, second-run call, non-striker response, acceleration, sprint speed, turn radius, turn time, bat carry, grounding the bat, sliding/dive-to-crease, crease-arrival time, run completion and run-out checks at both ends. Both batters must be represented in world space. Explain how the fielding position, ball speed, pickup/throw time and player call affect the decision. Include single, two, three, short run, run-out and boundary-with-running cases. Design the mobile control layout so running is clear and does not overlap swing controls.

J. Define cricket rules comprehensively

Specify five-over innings, six legal balls per over, innings role swap, chase target and match tie. Define bowled, caught, LBW, run out, stumped and hit wicket; optional dismissals must be clearly marked as later scope. Define wide, no-ball, byes, leg byes and free hit under one consistent limited-overs ruleset. Define over notation, bowler figures, batter score, extras, fall of wickets and scorecard. Define four and six boundary logic, ground contact, ball crossing rope, fielder contact with boundary, catch at boundary, overthrow and running runs. Define LBW using predicted ball path and bat-before-pad precedence. Avoid contradictory cricket-law interpretations; state any simplified house rules transparently.

K. Define UI/UX in detail

Design main menu, matchmaking, ready/toss, field setup, in-match HUD, batting controls, bowling controls, timing feedback, result/scorecard, pause/settings, reconnect and tutorial. Use reference-inspired broadcast-style cricket UI with modern hierarchy and clear safe areas. The ball corridor and striker must never be hidden by buttons. Include left-handed control layout, simplified/advanced control presets, text plus icons, accessibility settings, sound/haptics and network indicator. Specify all control states and their visual feedback.

L. Define multiplayer and technical architecture (without code)

Specify client/server responsibilities, authoritative match service, matchmaking, room coordinator, rules engine, deterministic ball simulation, batting/fielding/running modules, replication, interpolation, prediction, reconciliation, latency compensation, reconnect, replay, telemetry and anti-cheat. Server must own score and outcome. Clients send intent inputs only; they cannot declare a six, wicket, catch or run. Define state machine, conceptual entities, event messages, tick strategy and security boundaries. Describe how a live ball resolves if one client disconnects.

M. Produce a complete asset inventory and prompts

Create a production asset list with unique IDs, asset type, purpose, camera/readability requirements, model/texture/rig/animation needs, LOD needs, variants, dependencies and priority. Include at minimum:

